  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 256 AM EST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nEarly morning RAP analysis and GOES water vapor imagery depict two \ndeep upper level lows over the eastern half of North America, one \nover Manitoba and another over Maine. Upper Michigan is situated \nbetween these two large scale features, with the axis of a shortwave \nridge just to the east spanning from James Bay through Lower \nMichigan. At the surface, a weak warm front extends from the \nManitoba low across the western half of Lake Superior into the \nwestern UP. A few weak radar echos are noted in the west with this \nfeature, but thus far have not seen any precipitation reported \novernight as model soundings continue to depict a sizable layer of \ndry air in the lowest 3 kft of the atmosphere. Still, with a warm \nlayer just above the surface and surface temperatures mostly below \nfreezing, can't rule out some light freezing rain creating a light \nglaze in a few locations through later this morning if any \nprecipitation is able to eventually overcome the dry air and reach \nthe ground. Light rain will be possible across the eastern UP into \nthis afternoon as the weak frontal boundary continues eastward, with \nmost of the area warming into the 40s this afternoon.\n\nA trailing cold front will cross the UP tonight and bring about a \ntransition to a lake effect precipitation regime in the northwest \nwind snowbelts from Sunday into Monday. Expect some light snow over \nthe western UP as early as Sunday morning, but precipitation type \nwill likely stay rain in the east through most of the day on Sunday \nas temperatures once again climb to around 40, only changing over to \nsnow Sunday night into early Monday. Moisture continues to look \nlimited and does not extent very far into the DGZ per latest model \nsoundings, thus expect snow accumulations to remain minimal through \nMonday. Any minor travel impacts through early Monday will be \nfurther mitigated by relatively warm surface temperatures. Lake \neffect showers will taper off from west to east through the day on \nMonday as troughing moves off to the east. Then expect a stretch of \nrather benign weather through the rest of the week as a quasi-zonal \nflow pattern develops over the northern tier of the CONUS. A few \nclippers passing north of the area on Tuesday and again on Thursday \nmay bring occasional chances for precipitation, but chances remain \nat 30% or less for now and no significant precipitation is expected. \nTemperatures will stay above normal from Tuesday onward with highs \ngenerally in the 40s each afternoon and ranging from the 20s inland \nto the 30s near the lakeshores each night.",
